;;; Ein — feature demo
;;;
;;; Two `(or …)` disjuncts of one rule, asking **different** negative
;;; questions — the shape where one candidate can be parked on the boundary
;;; while another with the same bindings fires from the queue.
;;;
;;; `_seen` is keyed on `(binding_key, guards)`, not on the disjunct
;;; (S1.22.0), so both disjuncts of a match are enqueued: the guarded one
;;; parks until the closure quiesces, the unguarded one fires immediately.
;;; When they share a binding key the parked one is already *fired* by the
;;; time the boundary reaches it, and the round drops it rather than admitting
;;; a second derivation of the same conclusion.
;;;
;;; Every other fixture in the corpus leaves that branch untaken — it is the
;;; one path in `admit_from_boundary` that no zebra, no `features/*` and no
;;; `stdlib` program reaches — and M1a
;;; [S1a.6.12](../../plans/m1a_rust/p1a.6_performance/s1a.6.12_boundary_and_snapshot.md)
;;; made a round's cost depend on it: ein.rs skips the per-candidate `fired`
;;; probe on programs where no two entries *can* share a key, and this is the
;;; fixture that says what happens when they can.
;;;
;;; Expected: `touched` for both people, one per person, each derived once.
;;;   Alice — from the second disjunct, `(likes Alice Bob)` holds.
;;;   Bob    — from the first, nothing says `(likes Bob Bob)`.

(maybe-open Person)

(is-a Person T)
(is-a Alice  Person)
(is-a Bob    Person)

(likes Alice Bob)

;; The two disjuncts bind `?a` the same way and differ only in what they ask
;; of the world: one an `(absent …)` guard, the other a positive premise.
(rule maybe-open (?Person)
  :match  (or (and (is-a ?a ?Person) (absent (likes ?a Bob)))
              (and (is-a ?a ?Person) (likes ?a Bob)))
  :assert (touched ?a)
  :why    "{?a} is either liked-by-nobody-named-Bob or likes Bob"
  :priority 200)
